WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:04.330
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:09.290
Ever had an experience when you shot an almost
perfect photograph of for instance, an amazing

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:13.710
landscape but unfortunately it was littered
with unwanted objects.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:18.440
If only we had an algorithm that could perform
image inpainting, in other words, delete a

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:22.090
small part of an image and have it automatically
filled in!

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:25.119
So let's have a look at NVIDIA's AI-based
solution.

00:00:25.119 --> 00:00:29.240
On the left, you see the white regions that
are given to the algorithm to correct, and

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:31.800
on the right, you see the corrected images.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.600
So it works amazingly well, but the question
is why?

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.570
This is an established research field, so
what new can an AI-based approach bring to

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:40.570
the table?

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:44.950
Well, traditional non-learning approaches
either try to fill these holes in with other

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:49.770
pixels from the same image that have similar
neighborhoods, copy-paste something similar

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:56.410
if you will, or they try to record the distribution
of pixel colors and try to fill in something

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:57.820
using that knowledge.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:02.000
And here comes the important part - none of
these traditional approaches have an intuitive

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.710
understanding of the contents of the image.

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:09.530
And that is the main value proposition of
the neural network-based learning techniques!

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:14.210
This work also borrows from earlier artistic
style transfer methods to make sure that not

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:19.600
only the content, but the style of the inpainted
regions also match the original image.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.619
It is also remarkable that this new method
works with images that are devoid of symmetries

00:01:24.619 --> 00:01:30.470
and can also deal with cases where we cut
out really crazy, irregularly shaped holes.

00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:35.810
Of course, like every good piece of research
work, it has to be compared to previous algorithms.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:39.930
As you can see here, the quality of different
techniques is measured against a reference

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:44.520
output, and it is quite clear that this new
method produces more convincing results than

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:45.909
its competitors.

00:01:45.909 --> 00:01:51.110
For reference, PatchMatch is a landmark paper
from almost 10 years ago that still represents

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:53.970
the state of the art for non learning-based
techniques.

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:58.130
The paper contains a ton more of these comparisons,
so make sure to have a look.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:02.490
Without doubt, this is going to be an invaluable
tool for artists in the future.

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:08.039
In fact, in this very series, we use Photoshop's
built-in image inpainting tool on a daily

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:10.890
basis, so this will make our lives much easier.

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:11.930
Loving it.

